WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.820
A heartfelt speech, a somber agreement, a
prank involving zombies — it’s moments

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:10.510
like these that make The Big Bang Theory legendary,
even years after its final wrap.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:14.800
These are the show’s biggest moments ranked
from good to best!

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.689
In the early seasons of "The Big Bang Theory,"
it's easy to wonder why Leonard lives with

00:00:18.689 --> 00:00:22.429
Sheldon in the first place, given how difficult
he can be.

00:00:22.429 --> 00:00:26.890
Leonard answers this question in Season 3's
"The Staircase Implementation" when he tells

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.919
Penny the story of how he became Sheldon's
roommate.

00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:34.220
When Leonard started his job as a researcher
at Caltech, he needed to rent an apartment

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:35.220
nearby.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.860
He met Sheldon when the latter was sub-letting
his apartment, as his previous roommate Sebastian

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.190
could no longer stand to live with him.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:47.160
At that time, Sheldon made Leonard answer
a bunch of ridiculous questions before reluctantly

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.420
allowing him to move in.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.110
The two didn't get along at first, and Leonard
avoided having to hang out with Sheldon, preferring

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:58.440
instead to conduct home experiments with Howard,
played by Simon Helberg, and Raj, played by

00:00:58.440 --> 00:00:59.510
Kunal Nayyar.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:04.540
But one such experiment quickly turned dangerous
when the rocket fuel Leonard had created started

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:05.540
malfunctioning.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:10.320
It was Sheldon's quick thinking that prevented
the fuel from exploding in their apartment.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.370
Leonard felt indebted to Sheldon, and the
two quickly became good friends thereafter.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.960
"You're welcome."

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.149
The most important way in which Sheldon retains
power in his friendship with Leonard is through

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:22.200
the elaborate "Roommate Agreement."

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:26.390
Signed by Leonard when he first moved in,
the agreement contains elaborate rules for

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.889
the code of conduct between him and Sheldon
as roommates and friends.

00:01:30.889 --> 00:01:34.469
Naturally, the agreement is heavily skewed
in Sheldon's favor.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:39.499
But Leonard's one-time girlfriend Priya, played
by Aarti Mann, isn't so easily controlled

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:41.469
by the paradigms of the agreement.

00:01:41.469 --> 00:01:45.680
Priya's a lawyer and doesn't appreciate the
way Sheldon rigged the agreement to give himself

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:47.280
power over Leonard.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.499
In "The Agreement Dissection" in Season 4,
Priya argues around the clauses in the contract,

00:01:52.499 --> 00:01:54.289
much to Leonard's delight.

00:01:54.289 --> 00:01:58.659
Sheldon then threatens to expose Priya's relationship
with Leonard to her disapproving parents if

00:01:58.659 --> 00:02:01.759
the original terms of the agreement aren't
reinstated.

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.819
At Priya's urging, Leonard concedes to Sheldon's
demands, leaving him once again the ruler

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:09.450
of their little apartment kingdom.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.060
Sheldon never shows much interest in following
the dictates of conventional society.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.280
His genius intellect and antisocial nature
often combine to create a perfect storm of

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:20.920
insufferable behavior.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.920
And it usually falls on Leonard to keep Sheldon
in line during his most eccentric moments.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:30.810
One of the biggest instances of this occurs
in Season 3 with "The Einstein Approximation,"

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:35.430
in which Sheldon has been stuck on a problem
at work regarding the behavior of electrons.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:40.370
This leads him to try increasingly bizarre
ways to wrap his brain around the issue while

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:44.290
forgoing the usual, pesky human weaknesses
of food and sleep.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:48.200
It's left to Leonard to try to keep a handle
on the sleep-deprived lunatic his roommate

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.370
becomes.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:54.200
Sheldon works all night on a whiteboard trying
unsuccessfully to solve his problem, which

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.579
he promptly tosses into the street the next
morning, causing a car crash.

00:02:58.579 --> 00:03:03.470
Sheldon also lays out intricate patterns of
marbles in the living room in a bid to understand

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:08.120
the movement of electrons, which causes Penny
and Leonard to slip and fall painfully.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.810
His crowning moment, however, is when Leonard
is forced to follow Sheldon into a kid's ball

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:17.940
pit that the latter swims around in like Scrooge
McDuck, shouting "Bazinga!"

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.940
repeatedly.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:23.150
Though Leonard is devoted to taking care of
Sheldon like an older brother would, this

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.299
often becomes a sore spot for Leonard's friends,
who think he's being taken advantage of.

00:03:28.299 --> 00:03:32.540
Leonard has a major reckoning in the Season
5 episode "The Friendship Contraction."

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:33.670
"You know what?

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.939
I'm sick of the roommate agreement."

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:42.420
After a long day of Sheldon being his usual
demanding self, Leonard invokes Clause 209

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:46.170
of the Roommate Agreement, nullifying the
"friendship" aspect of their relationship

00:03:46.170 --> 00:03:49.000
and reducing them to mere roommates.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:53.580
At first, Sheldon tries to replace Leonard
with his other friends, but it quickly becomes

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.900
apparent that no one has the patience to deal
with him.

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.290
Desperate to have Leonard back but loath to
admit defeat, Sheldon resorts to turning off

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.170
the entire building's lights in an effort
to make Leonard need his help, but the ruse

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.420
doesn't work.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.830
Finally, Leonard agrees to reinstate the "friendship"
clause in return for an annual "Leonard's

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:12.830
Day."

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:18.269
"We set aside a day to celebrate all your
contributions to my life, both actual and

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:19.940
imagined by you."

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:24.430
One of the most accurate descriptions of Sheldon
Cooper is provided by Leonard when he tells

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.430
Penny,

00:04:25.430 --> 00:04:28.800
"The guy's one lab accident away from being
a supervillain."

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.180
Sheldon's Machiavellian tendencies are on
full display in "The Itchy Brain Simulation"

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:40.610
in Season 7 when Leonard reveals that a forgotten
DVD they'd once rented is seven years overdue.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:44.860
He makes a deal with Leonard whereby he must
wear an extremely itchy sweater under his

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:50.350
clothes until he manages to return the DVD
so he can appreciate how uncomfortable Sheldon

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.830
feels mentally whenever he's forced to deal
with unfinished business.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.750
Leonard agrees to the deal, and the rest of
the episode sees him growing increasingly

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:03.990
desperate as he continues to wear the sweater
while trying to find a way to return the DVD.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.889
In the end, it's revealed that Sheldon had
paid for the DVD years ago and was using the

00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:12.260
situation to teach Leonard a lesson about
responsibility.

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:16.479
The angry red welts on Leonard's body caused
by the sweater at the end of the episode show

00:05:16.479 --> 00:05:19.520
just how painful that lesson is.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:24.090
Sheldon's primary character trait throughout
most of "The Big Bang Theory" is his overwhelming

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.090
narcissism.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:29.250
He's the most important person in his life,
and he expects his friends to place him in

00:05:29.250 --> 00:05:34.790
a similarly important position in their lives,
as he amply demonstrates in Season 3 with

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:36.690
"The Large Hadron Collision."

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:41.560
When Leonard gets the opportunity to go to
Switzerland to observe the Large Hadron Collider,

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.509
he's thrilled to be able to take his girlfriend,
Penny, with him to celebrate Valentine's Day.

00:05:46.509 --> 00:05:51.100
When Sheldon hears about Leonard's trip, however,
he assumes his roommate would take him instead

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.100
of Penny.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.990
When he discovers that's not the case, he's
outraged.

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.460
Throughout the episode, Sheldon begs, pleads,
and schemes his way around convincing Leonard

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:00.849
to take him.

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:04.971
If there's one character in "The Big Bang
Theory" who seems incapable of changing, it's

00:06:04.971 --> 00:06:10.730
Sheldon Cooper, who's perennially convinced
that any problem is with everyone else, not

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:11.730
him.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:16.230
Incredibly, however, Sheldon does begin to
change after meeting Amy Farrah Fowler played

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.750
by Mayim Bialik.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:22.910
Although he's completely uninterested in romance
at first, Sheldon begins to date Amy as a

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:24.629
sort of experiment.

00:06:24.629 --> 00:06:29.020
He repeatedly insists they aren't romantically
involved, but over time, Sheldon begins to

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:34.060
realize he cares a great deal more for Amy
than he was initially prepared to admit.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.849
One of the earliest and most hilarious examples
of this occurs in Season 5's "The Pulled Groin

00:06:38.849 --> 00:06:39.940
Extrapolation."

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:44.690
At first, Sheldon seems completely indifferent
to the fact that Leonard and Amy had gone

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:48.970
to a party together—until he misconstrues
something Leonard says as a hint that they'd

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:51.560
gotten frisky and karate-chops him.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:52.620
"Why'd you do that?"

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:53.949
"To send a message.

00:06:53.949 --> 00:06:55.770
She is not for you!"

00:06:55.770 --> 00:07:00.860
The joy of Sheldon's characterization is in
watching someone with genius intellect inhabit

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.890
the body of a hyperactive man-child.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:08.630
He has no sense of social niceties, and Leonard
always acts as his reluctant caretaker.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:14.139
All those factors combine for a perfect storm
of hilarity in "The Bozeman Reaction" in Season

00:07:14.139 --> 00:07:15.139
3.

00:07:15.139 --> 00:07:18.830
After a night out, Leonard and Sheldon arrive
back at their apartment to discover that the

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:22.419
lock has been broken and their belongings
have been ransacked.

00:07:22.419 --> 00:07:26.909
Although the robbers didn't take anything
of material value, Sheldon becomes paranoid

00:07:26.909 --> 00:07:28.940
at the thought of another possible break-in.

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:34.240
Throughout the episode, he comes up with increasingly
elaborate anti-burglary alarms that end up

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.820
backfiring hilariously.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:41.160
Finally, Sheldon is forced to conclude that
he can no longer live in a city that's a cesspool

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:45.500
of crime and decides to relocate to a place
where there would be less chance of getting

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.500
murdered.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.240
"Is he quitting his job at the University?"

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.240
"Oh, no, he's going to telecommute.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.890
Everybody's really excited about it."

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.819
Throughout the ordeal, Leonard repeatedly
tries to reassure Sheldon that he's safe in

00:07:55.819 --> 00:07:59.849
their apartment, putting up with all of his
hysterics and ridiculous schemes.

00:07:59.849 --> 00:08:04.750
We often see Leonard taking care of Sheldon
and making concessions for his off-putting

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:08.880
behavior, to the extent that Leonard almost
comes across as a doormat.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.990
It's much rarer to see Sheldon taking care
of Leonard.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:16.271
But the truth is, despite taking his friendship
for granted most of the time, Sheldon does

00:08:16.271 --> 00:08:18.060
genuinely care for Leonard.

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:23.250
The sweetest example of his affection occurs
in Season 6's "The Decoupling Fluctuation."

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:28.180
While Leonard and Penny are dating, it's revealed
that Penny has secret doubts about their relationship.

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:32.659
When Sheldon learns of this, he becomes increasingly
agitated with the weight of the secret and

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:34.890
its potential impact on his friend.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:38.479
This tension causes Sheldon to wake Penny
up in the middle of the night to tell her

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:42.570
all the ways Leonard is a great guy and prime
boyfriend material.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:47.200
As the thoroughly exasperated Penny orders
Sheldon to leave, he finally drops his usual

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.450
arrogance and aloofness, and instead has a
marked humility that he reserves for only

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:54.170
the most special of occasions.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.290
"Please don't hurt my friend."

00:08:56.290 --> 00:09:00.640
After many years as Sheldon's roommate, Leonard
had gathered a vast collection of knowledge

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:01.880
about his friend.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.540
Some of that knowledge is actively uncomfortable,
like knowing Sheldon's bathroom schedule.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:11.640
But sometimes, Leonard's expert knowledge
of Sheldon's personal life proves invaluable.

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:15.760
Take the case of "The Panty Piñata Polarization"
in Season 2.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.370
After a long day of having to deal with Sheldon's
nonsense, Penny finally puts her foot down

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:23.380
and declares she will no longer follow his
rules.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:27.790
This results in Penny being "banished" until
she's willing to apologize and take a class

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:29.360
to improve her attitude.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.450
When Penny proves unrepentant and hostile,
it kickstarts an escalating series of pranks

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:39.110
between her and Sheldon that soon threatens
to spill over into violence.

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.430
That's when Leonard tells Penny that he knows
Sheldon's kryptonite.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.690
He gives her the phone number of Sheldon's
mother, Mary, who swiftly puts an end to the

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.940
war and forces Sheldon to apologize.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:54.110
Leonard and Sheldon can often come across
as immature and ill-equipped to handle adult

00:09:54.110 --> 00:09:55.110
life.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:59.220
But the fact remains that both are also top
researchers in their fields whose professional

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.750
reputations are quite distinguished.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:07.000
These two factors collide in an epic manner
in "The Cooper-Hofstadter Polarization" in

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.260
Season 1.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:12.660
Leonard and Sheldon write a research paper
together and are subsequently invited to present

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.310
it at a conference in front of their peers.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:18.990
Sheldon doesn't want to go because he doesn't
think the crowd at the conference has the

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.380
necessary genius to properly appreciate his
work.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:27.260
An indignant Leonard declares he'll be going
to the conference with or without his co-author.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:32.029
Sheldon refuses to allow Leonard to present
the paper alone and also refuses to accompany

00:10:32.029 --> 00:10:33.029
him.

00:10:33.029 --> 00:10:37.250
A long, petty fight ensues, culminating in
them airing out their dirty laundry in front

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.540
of the audience at the conference.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:44.340
Sheldon attempts to blow Leonard's brain apart
using his mind, which obviously doesn't work.

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.550
The whole ordeal later gets put up on the
internet as a lasting testament to Leonard

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:51.130
and Sheldon's bizarre relationship.

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:56.410
One of Sheldon's many quirks is his hypochondria,
which is aggravated by his genius intellect.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:11:02.089
He often envisions worst-case scenarios whenever
he has to undergo any sort of medical procedure.

00:11:02.089 --> 00:11:07.540
In the Season 8 episode, "The Septum Deviation,"
Sheldon's worries transfer over to Leonard.

00:11:07.540 --> 00:11:11.980
After a routine checkup, Leonard learns he
has a deviated septum that prevents him from

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:13.320
breathing properly.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:18.290
He decides to get a small surgery to correct
his condition, but Sheldon is appalled, believing

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:21.170
that it could lead to all sorts of complications.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:25.870
All through the episode, Sheldon remains extremely
panicky over his friend's surgery.

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:29.740
Things come to a head when the light goes
out in the middle of the operation, causing

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:34.610
Sheldon to run screaming into the room to
try to save Leonard and subsequently crash

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.540
into a glass door, breaking his nose.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:42.010
To the rest of the world, Sheldon Cooper might
seem like a humorless man who takes himself

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:43.430
way too seriously.

00:11:43.430 --> 00:11:47.839
But his closest friends know that Sheldon
has an impish sense of humor, and that he's

00:11:47.839 --> 00:11:52.880
more than willing to participate in all kinds
of hijinks with the right companions.

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:57.860
One of the most entertaining instances of
this occurs in Season 5's "The Good Guy Fluctuation."

00:11:57.860 --> 00:12:02.639
While working late in his office, Sheldon
is beset by a series of pranks by Raj and

00:12:02.639 --> 00:12:03.709
Howard.

00:12:03.709 --> 00:12:07.980
Sheldon manages to see through all of them
with his trademark air of superiority, only

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:12.600
to end up completely freaking out when Leonard
sneaks up behind him in a scary mask.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.699
The incident leaves a deep impression on Sheldon,
and he plots his revenge against the others.

00:12:17.699 --> 00:12:21.639
All through the episode, Sheldon tries to
play pranks on the others, but his lack of

00:12:21.639 --> 00:12:26.380
social awareness and the fact that the others
know he's coming make the pranks fail.

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:31.280
Finally, while Leonard is alone in their apartment
musing over some devastating news, Sheldon

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:36.350
pops up screaming from beneath his spot on
their couch dressed in zombie makeup for an

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.790
epically hilarious prank.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:43.210
Sheldon never tries to hide the fact that
he considers himself superior to his friends.

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:47.899
His dream since childhood was always to win
the Nobel Prize for physics.

00:12:47.899 --> 00:12:51.920
The big day finally arrives in the series
finale, "The Stockholm Syndrome."

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.570
Sheldon and Amy win a joint Nobel for their
research in super asymmetry.

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:00.910
On the stage after receiving his award, Sheldon
looks over at Leonard and his friends in the

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:06.210
audience and realizes he's no longer the same
man who years before dreamed only of bragging

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.450
about his genius before the world.

00:13:08.450 --> 00:13:12.500
Instead, Sheldon takes the opportunity to
thank his friends for helping him grow as

00:13:12.500 --> 00:13:14.889
a person and as a scientist.

00:13:14.889 --> 00:13:20.120
He calls each one and asks them to stand,
then in his most honest moment on the show,

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.120
says,

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.050
"I apologize if I haven't been the friend
you deserve."

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:30.870
"But I want you to know, in my way, I love
you all."

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:35.829
With that speech, Sheldon goes from man-child
to mature adult and acknowledges the almost

00:13:35.829 --> 00:13:40.360
superhuman patience that Leonard and the others
had displayed toward him over the years.

